import{c as o,$ as r,Q as s,j as n,g as t,o as i,n as l,m as c}from"./chunks/framework.Ce_49mA8.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Service Cycle","description":"Step-by-step end-to-end guide to the service workshop pipeline in OmniCraft ERP — from client registration and price lists to service orders, warehouse spare parts write-offs, and final cash settlement.","frontmatter":{"title":"Service Cycle","description":"Step-by-step end-to-end guide to the service workshop pipeline in OmniCraft ERP — from client registration and price lists to service orders, warehouse spare parts write-offs, and final cash settlement.","datePublished":"2026-11-15","dateModified":"2026-11-24","order":10},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/spaces/services/cycle.md","filePath":"docs/spaces/services/cycle.md","lastUpdated":1795478400000}'),d={name:"docs/spaces/services/cycle.md"},p={id:"service-cycle-step-by-step-workshop-guide",tabindex:"-1"};function h(u,e,m,g,f,v){const a=r("DocIcon");return s(),n("div",null,[t("h1",p,[i(a,{name:"workflow"}),e[0]||(e[0]=l(" Service Cycle: Step-by-Step Workshop Guide ",-1)),e[1]||(e[1]=t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#service-cycle-step-by-step-workshop-guide","aria-label":'Permalink to "Service Cycle: Step-by-Step Workshop Guide"'},"​",-1))]),e[2]||(e[2]=c('<p>Running a service and repair workshop is fundamentally different from serial batch production. In production manufacturing, the artisan produces identical items following a static recipe. In service and maintenance, every incoming order is unique: one tool requires simple calibration, another needs urgent replacement of worn assemblies, and a third demands thorough diagnostics to trace elusive hidden defects.</p><p>When workshop records are kept on scraps of paper, agreements get lost in chat threads, and spare parts are deducted by memory, the business incurs silent leaks. Forgotten advances cause friction with clients, unaccounted seals and bearings distort warehouse balances, and true repair profitability ends up far below expectations.</p><p>In <strong>OmniCraft ERP</strong>, the Services workspace organizes your shop as a disciplined step-by-step assembly line. Every physical repair milestone is directly wired to accounting: the client profile tracks relationship history, the price list enforces labor standards, opening an order logs customer advances in the cash register, and closing the ticket writes off replacement parts and locks in net commercial profit.</p><hr><h2 id="physical-workshop-case-study" tabindex="-1">Physical Workshop Case Study <a class="header-anchor" href="#physical-workshop-case-study" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Physical Workshop Case Study&quot;">​</a></h2><p>To trace the pipeline on realistic figures, let us examine a standard job in an electromechanical repair shop: <strong>&quot;Comprehensive maintenance and brush gear overhaul of a heavy rotary hammer&quot;</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Customer:</strong> Alex Voronov (private general contractor), recurring client.</li><li><strong>Agreed Services Scope:</strong><ul><li>Power tool diagnostics and inspection — fixed tariff <code>$8.00</code> (or <code>800 ₽</code>);</li><li>Preventive disassembly, commutator cleaning, and alignment — standardized job for <code>1.5</code> hours at a master hourly rate of <code>$12.00/h</code> plus <code>15%</code> shop overhead.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Parts and Consumables from Warehouse:</strong><ul><li>Carbon brush assembly with springs — <code>$5.50</code> (at current inventory cost);</li><li>High-temperature gearbox grease and industrial cleaner — <code>$2.50</code>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Total Agreed Order Price:</strong> <code>$50.00</code>.</li><li><strong>Customer Advance (Prepayment):</strong> <code>$20.00</code> upon handing over the tool.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="step-1-registering-the-customer-in-the-directory" tabindex="-1">Step 1. Registering the Customer in the Directory <a class="header-anchor" href="#step-1-registering-the-customer-in-the-directory" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 1. Registering the Customer in the Directory&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every high-standard repair business begins with organized contacts. Before accepting equipment into service, record the client&#39;s profile.</p><p>Navigate to the <strong>&quot;Clients&quot;</strong> tab and click the <strong>&quot;New Client&quot;</strong> button:</p><ol><li><strong>Entering Contact Details:</strong> Specify the client&#39;s name (<em>&quot;Alex Voronov&quot;</em>) and phone number. The software automatically applies the phone formatting mask.</li><li><strong>Communication Channels and Notes:</strong> Add preferred messenger handles for fast approvals of hidden damage and include workshop notes (e.g., <em>&quot;Finishing crew foreman, recurring customer&quot;</em>).</li><li>Click the <strong>&quot;Save&quot;</strong> button.</li></ol><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Unified Customer Card</p><p>OmniCraft ERP binds all work orders directly to client IDs. In seconds, you can review repair history, verify warranty deadlines, and evaluate cumulative revenue generated by any customer.</p></div><hr><h2 id="step-2-building-the-service-catalog-and-labor-rates" tabindex="-1">Step 2. Building the Service Catalog and Labor Rates <a class="header-anchor" href="#step-2-building-the-service-catalog-and-labor-rates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 2. Building the Service Catalog and Labor Rates&quot;">​</a></h2><p>To avoid calculating standard job prices from scratch during every customer check-in, maintain an organized service price list.</p><p>Navigate to the <strong>&quot;Services&quot;</strong> tab and click the <strong>&quot;Add Service&quot;</strong> button:</p><p>The system supports two distinct pricing models:</p><ol><li><strong>Fixed Tariff:</strong> Enter a title (<em>&quot;Diagnostics &amp; Inspection&quot;</em>) and an exact price — <code>$8.00</code>. This mode fits standard routine tasks with predictable scope.</li><li><strong>Standardized Labor Hours:</strong> For complex restorations and diagnostic tear-downs, specify standard labor time (e.g., <code>1.5</code> hours). The software pulls your hourly master rate from Settings (<code>$12.00/h</code>), adds your workshop overhead percentage (<code>15%</code>), and computes an objective price:</li></ol><div class="formula-hero-card"><div class="formula-card-top"><span class="formula-badge">Service Price List</span><div class="formula-heading">Calculated Rate for Standardized Labor</div></div><div class="formula-equation-box"><div class="math-formula-display"><span class="math-var">Service Price</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-num">1.5 h</span><span class="math-op">×</span><span class="math-num">$12.00</span><span class="math-op">×</span><span class="math-num">1.15</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-result">$20.70</span></div></div></div><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Workshop Foreman Tip: Shielding Margins via Overhead</p><p>Never estimate repairs purely on spent bench hours. The hourly rate must absorb diagnostic test bench amortization, electricity, workplace lighting, and premises rent. Applying workshop overhead percentages in OmniCraft ERP ensures you never work at a hidden loss.</p></div><hr><h2 id="step-3-opening-a-service-order-and-logging-advance" tabindex="-1">Step 3. Opening a Service Order and Logging Advance <a class="header-anchor" href="#step-3-opening-a-service-order-and-logging-advance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 3. Opening a Service Order and Logging Advance&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Once the tool is inspected and the job scope is agreed with the customer, open an official service order.</p><p>Navigate to the <strong>&quot;Orders&quot;</strong> tab and click the <strong>&quot;New Service Order&quot;</strong> button:</p><ol><li><strong>Order Description:</strong> State the core scope (<em>&quot;Rotary hammer maintenance: gearbox inspection and brush replacement&quot;</em>).</li><li><strong>Client Link:</strong> Select the customer from the directory (<em>Alex Voronov</em>).</li><li><strong>Total Amount:</strong> Enter the total agreed order amount including spare parts — <code>$50.00</code>.</li><li><strong>Prepayment:</strong> Enter the received advance payment — <code>$20.00</code>.</li><li>Click the <strong>&quot;Save&quot;</strong> button.</li></ol><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Automatic Cash Inflow Recording (DDS)</p><p>You never need to open the cash register module and manually log the money receipt. OmniCraft ERP enforces end-to-end ledger integrity: the exact second you save the order, a cash receipt of <code>+$20.00</code> is generated in the <strong>&quot;DDS&quot;</strong> tab under the category <em>&quot;Service advance payment&quot;</em> tied to this order number.</p></div><hr><h2 id="step-4-picking-spare-parts-and-warehouse-deduction" tabindex="-1">Step 4. Picking Spare Parts and Warehouse Deduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#step-4-picking-spare-parts-and-warehouse-deduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 4. Picking Spare Parts and Warehouse Deduction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>During repairs, the technician picks necessary assemblies and chemical supplies from inventory.</p><ol><li>Navigate to the <strong>&quot;Materials&quot;</strong> tab (Warehouse).</li><li>Locate the items in stock: <em>&quot;Carbon brush set&quot;</em> (1 pc) and <em>&quot;Gearbox grease&quot;</em> (0.1 kg).</li><li>Process a material write-off stating the reason: <em>&quot;Consumed in service order&quot;</em>.</li></ol><div class="info custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Moving Average Cost (MAC)</p><p>Spare parts are written off strictly at their actual inventory valuation using the Moving Average Cost (MAC) method. If brush batches arrived at different purchase rates, the system writes off the part at the true weighted average cost, preventing distorted repair expense calculations.</p></div><hr><h2 id="step-5-completing-the-work-and-closing-the-order" tabindex="-1">Step 5. Completing the Work and Closing the Order <a class="header-anchor" href="#step-5-completing-the-work-and-closing-the-order" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 5. Completing the Work and Closing the Order&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The technician has cleaned the commutator, seated the new carbon brushes, repacked the gearbox with fresh lubricant, and load-tested the tool on the bench. The machine is ready for pickup.</p><p>Under the <strong>&quot;Orders&quot;</strong> tab, locate the ticket and click the <strong>&quot;Complete Order&quot;</strong> button (green checkmark icon on the order card):</p><ol><li><strong>Confirmation Prompt:</strong> The system requests verification: <em>&quot;Complete the order and record the final payment to DDS?&quot;</em>.</li><li><strong>What Happens Under the Hood:</strong><ul><li>The system computes full job cost (written-off parts <code>$8.00</code> + labor expense <code>$18.00</code> + overhead <code>$3.90</code> = <code>$29.90</code>);</li><li>The program calculates remaining client balance: <code>$50.00 − $20.00 = $30.00</code>;</li><li>The second cash inflow entry of <code>+$30.00</code> is posted to the workshop cash ledger (<strong>&quot;DDS&quot;</strong> tab) under <em>&quot;Final service payment&quot;</em>;</li><li>The ticket transitions to the permanent <code>COMPLETED</code> state and enters your historical archive.</li></ul></li></ol><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Canceling an Order: Safe Refund Workflow</p><p>If teardown reveals that the motor armature is scorched beyond economic repair, click the <strong>&quot;Cancel Order&quot;</strong> button (red cross icon). The program offers to refund the customer&#39;s advance and automatically generates an expense transaction in DDS stating the refund justification.</p></div><hr><h2 id="step-6-financial-settlement-and-closing-the-loop" tabindex="-1">Step 6. Financial Settlement and Closing the Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#step-6-financial-settlement-and-closing-the-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Step 6. Financial Settlement and Closing the Loop&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With the refurbished tool returned and full payment settled, the commercial loop closes completely:</p><ul><li><strong>Cash Register (DDS):</strong> Two clear inflows stand on record: advance payment <code>+$20.00</code> and completion payment <code>+$30.00</code>. The register logged precisely <code>$50.00</code>.</li><li><strong>Net Deal Margin:</strong> Subtracting total job cost (parts, labor, and overhead) from gross order revenue reveals net operational margin:</li></ul><div class="formula-hero-card"><div class="formula-card-top"><span class="formula-badge">Financial Outcome</span><div class="formula-heading">Net Profit from Service Order</div></div><div class="formula-equation-box"><div class="math-formula-display"><span class="math-var">Net Profit</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-num">$50.00</span><span class="math-op">−</span><span class="math-num">$8.00</span><span class="math-op">−</span><span class="math-num">$18.00</span><span class="math-op">−</span><span class="math-num">$3.90</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-result">$20.10</span></div></div></div><ul><li><strong>Analytics:</strong> Total service revenue charts, average repair ticket size, and customer revenue breakdown update automatically.</li><li><strong>Balance Sheet (Capital):</strong> Inventory asset valuation decreases by parts cost (<code>$8.00</code>), while liquid cash balances increase by the full <code>$50.00</code>. Your workshop net equity expands by the earned net profit.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="service-resource-movement-matrix" tabindex="-1">Service Resource Movement Matrix <a class="header-anchor" href="#service-resource-movement-matrix" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Service Resource Movement Matrix&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Service Milestone</th><th style="text-align:left;">Software Tab</th><th style="text-align:left;">Material Inventory Flow</th><th style="text-align:left;">Cash Ledger (DDS)</th><th style="text-align:left;">Economic Outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>1. Intake &amp; Client</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Clients</td><td style="text-align:left;">No movement</td><td style="text-align:left;">No cash entry</td><td style="text-align:left;">Contact and diagnostic defect record stored</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>2. Price List</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Services</td><td style="text-align:left;">No movement</td><td style="text-align:left;">No cash entry</td><td style="text-align:left;">Labor standards and base pricing locked</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>3. Order Intake</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Orders</td><td style="text-align:left;">Scope reserved</td><td style="text-align:left;">Advance received (<code>+$20</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">Order accepted, legal commitment booked</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>4. Parts Picking</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Materials</td><td style="text-align:left;">Brushes and grease written off (MAC)</td><td style="text-align:left;">No cash entry</td><td style="text-align:left;">Spare parts transfer into job cost</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>5. Closing Order</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Orders</td><td style="text-align:left;">Deduction finished</td><td style="text-align:left;">Final balance received (<code>+$30</code>)</td><td style="text-align:left;">Ticket closed, tool handed over to client</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>6. Balance Update</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">Analytics / Capital</td><td style="text-align:left;">Warehouse value reduced</td><td style="text-align:left;">Gross revenue accounted</td><td style="text-align:left;">Net margin booked, net equity grows</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Master Craftsman Advice: Separate Advances from Final Payment</p><p>Never begin tool teardowns or order custom spare parts without securing a customer deposit. Advance payments protect material expenditures and guarantee customer commitment. The automated &quot;Order ➔ Cash&quot; linkage in OmniCraft ERP ensures no customer prepayment ever slips through the cracks of daily workshop expenses.</p></div><hr><h2 id="troubleshooting-common-pitfalls" tabindex="-1">Troubleshooting &amp; Common Pitfalls <a class="header-anchor" href="#troubleshooting-common-pitfalls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Troubleshooting &amp; Common Pitfalls&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="faq-grid"><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">What should I do if hidden damage is uncovered and repair costs increase?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Open the work order for editing and append necessary additional operations from the catalog or warehouse parts. The system automatically recalculates the total invoice balance, prompting for the revised balance upon tool collection.</p></div></details><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">How do I correctly handle a customer cancellation after an advance has been paid?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Click the red cancel button on the work order row. Confirm the action: the system automatically logs a refund expense transaction in the Cash Flow ledger and returns all reserved spare parts to active warehouse inventory.</p></div></details><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">What if a required replacement part is out of stock in the workshop?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Enter an intake receipt under the Warehouse tab for the purchased replacement item. The component immediately becomes available for assignment and consumption within the active work order at exact procurement cost.</p></div></details></div>',51))])}const k=o(d,[["render",h]]);export{b as __pageData,k as default};
